WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:07.800
Wishonia On one planet, citizens allocate the budget directly, lobbyists don't exist, and disease was eradicated four thousand

2
00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:15.600
years ago. It uses the same technology you have. The difference is they pointed it at biology instead of each other.

3
00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:21.000
Hello again. Wishonia here. I'm from a planet also called Wishonia.

4
00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:25.400
We stopped having wars four thousand two hundred and ninety-seven years ago.

5
00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:34.880
Not because we evolved morally. We bribed everyone. Our version of your one percent treaty redirected resources from killing

6
00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:43.220
each other to keeping each other alive, and once the incentives aligned, peace stopped being a moral aspiration and became

7
00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:47.640
a financial position. Nobody had to become a better person.

8
00:00:48.220 --> 00:00:52.160
The money just circulated until war stopped being profitable.

9
00:00:52.980 --> 00:01:01.560
Morality is debatable. Spreadsheets are not. This chapter is the answer key to a test you haven't taken yet.

10
00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:12.060
How My Planet Works. On Wishonia, we don't have wars (ran the numbers, not profitable), disease (fixed them all, took about

11
00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:19.520
fifty of your years once we started trying), death from old age (optional now, most people opt out the way you opt out of

12
00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:26.600
email newsletters), or committees deciding who gets healthcare (we tried that for a while, and it went about as well as

13
00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:31.620
you'd expect, which is to say: badly, slowly, and with a lot of catering).

14
00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:41.280
We do have A.I.-optimized resource allocation that maximizes marginal median happiness through wishocracy, treatments tested

15
00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:49.820
simultaneously on willing participants instead of serially on unwilling lab animals, life expectancy of "until you're bored,"

16
00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:57.940
and an economy where the profit motive and the survival motive are the same motive, which turns out to be the only economic

17
00:01:57.940 --> 00:02:03.890
innovation that matters. You have the same atoms we had. You have the same physics.

18
00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:12.520
You have better computers than we had at this stage. The only difference is we stopped spending resources on explosion technology

19
00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:15.700
around the time you were still hitting each other with rocks.

20
00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:23.540
You kept going. You got very good at it. Nobody on Wishonia disputes that you are the best rock-hitters the universe has

21
00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:27.960
ever produced. We just question whether that's the thing you want to be best at.

22
00:02:29.540 --> 00:02:37.020
What the Optimized World Looks Like. The Three Supers. Depression gets cured.

23
00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:44.000
Not "managed" with a monthly subscription to sadness pills that list "suicidal thoughts" as a side effect, which is like

24
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:51.200
selling a fire extinguisher that's also a flamethrower. *Cured.* Precision therapies that actually work because you tested

25
00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:56.460
them on thousands of willing participants instead of twelve college students who needed beer money.

26
00:02:57.260 --> 00:03:04.900
But "cured" is the floor, not the ceiling. Once you understand suffering well enough to end it, you understand it well enough

27
00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:13.000
to replace it with something better. Your current emotional range runs from "actively terrible" to "briefly okay."

28
00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:20.200
This is not because the universe is stingy with happiness. It's because your genes built a motivational system where contentment

29
00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:27.000
is punished (content animals stop foraging and get eaten) and satisfaction expires by design.

30
00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:34.660
The hedonic treadmill exists because evolution needs you perpetually dissatisfied, not because the universe hates you.

31
00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:41.940
Although the evidence is mixed. On Wishonia, we called this a bug, not the human condition.

32
00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:50.320
We patched it. The patch: gradients of intelligent bliss. Not the dopamine fog of drugs, where you feel great about nothing.

33
00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:59.100
Information-sensitive well-being, where your emotional range runs from "deeply good" to "transcendently good," and every

34
00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:05.620
feeling still carries real information about the world. You still prefer a great meal to a mediocre one.

35
00:04:05.980 --> 00:04:13.500
The mediocre one just doesn't ruin your afternoon. Your worst Tuesday feels better than your current best birthday, not

36
00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:18.230
because you lowered your standards, but because you rebuilt the hardware that sets them.

37
00:04:18.500 --> 00:04:25.080
This is Super-Wellbeing. Aging slows, then reverses. Death becomes optional.

38
00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:30.230
Death currently holds two point three stars on Yelp. "Would not recommend," writes everyone.

39
00:04:30.620 --> 00:04:33.780
"The ambiance was terrible and I couldn't leave a complaint because I was dead."

40
00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:44.120
This is Super-Longevity. A.I. optimized for "maximize median health and happiness" solves problems human scientists couldn't

41
00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:48.980
touch. Not because humans are dumb (well, partly), but because the A.I.

42
00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:53.480
can run a billion simulations while you're still arguing about how to pronounce "gif."

43
00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:58.640
Hard G, by the way. On Wishonia, we settled this three thousand years ago.

44
00:04:58.900 --> 00:05:03.590
It was harder than curing cancer but more urgent. This is Super-Intelligence.

45
00:05:05.020 --> 00:05:10.640
Biology Becomes Software. Your body runs Body O.S. twelve point three.

46
00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:17.060
Updates every Tuesday. Not like your phone updates, where "update" means "we moved the buttons and broke the thing you liked."

47
00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:25.540
Actual updates. Fixes. Robot lab assistants perform experiments one thousand times faster than humans (they don't need lunch

48
00:05:25.540 --> 00:05:32.160
breaks, bathroom breaks, or existential crises about whether their research matters, which is what human scientists have

49
00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:39.260
at three A.M. instead of sleep). Drug companies design, synthesize, and validate new treatments over weekends.

50
00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:45.840
The weekend is now the most productive period in pharmaceutical history, which is ironic given that it used to be the least

51
00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:54.880
productive period in every industry. Today's body patches include cancer immunity, Alzheimer's prevention, perfect pitch,

52
00:05:55.400 --> 00:06:00.160
airplane food digestion, cryptocurrency comprehension, and jazz appreciation.

53
00:06:00.920 --> 00:06:06.620
Don't like your genetics? Change them. The only limits are imagination and noise ordinances.

54
00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:16.550
A Day in the Optimized Life. Here's what Tuesday looks like when the Three Supers are normal: Six A.M.: Wake up naturally.

55
00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:21.550
Body O.S. twelve point three optimized your sleep. You feel excellent.

56
00:06:21.980 --> 00:06:26.980
No alarm needed. Your circadian rhythm works correctly for the first time in human history.

57
00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:33.160
Seven A.M.: Breakfast. Outcome Labels tell you exactly how each food affects your body.

58
00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:36.800
Dark chocolate improves cognitive function by twenty-three percent.

59
00:06:37.240 --> 00:06:43.280
You adjust your I.Q. to eighty for morning news (otherwise it's unwatchable), then two hundred for work.

60
00:06:43.300 --> 00:06:50.020
Nine A.M. Work on your passion project. Today: reverse-engineering nostalgia so you can feel it about things that haven't

61
00:06:50.020 --> 00:06:57.080
happened yet. Yesterday you were engineering a sunset that lasts exactly as long as the conversation needs it to.

62
00:06:58.100 --> 00:07:06.900
Nobody pays you for this. Your daily wishes U.B.I. deposit arrived at midnight via smart contract (no paperwork, no means

63
00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:13.580
test, no bureaucrat, no I.R.S.). You work because you want to, not because landlords exist.

64
00:07:14.380 --> 00:07:20.980
Your Wishocracy allocation takes three seconds: five pairwise slides (medical research vs.

65
00:07:21.020 --> 00:07:26.040
military? eighty-five to fifteen. Education vs. drug enforcement?

66
00:07:26.260 --> 00:07:33.420
ninety to ten. Infrastructure vs. fossil fuel subsidies? Easy.), and the algorithm handles the rest.

67
00:07:34.580 --> 00:07:38.590
Twelve P.M. Lunch with your friend who died last year. They got better.

68
00:07:39.640 --> 00:07:46.860
Your great-great-grandmother (one hundred and fifty, looks twenty-five) joins via hologram from her Mars artist residency.

69
00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:51.060
Your dog is technically a minor deity. Nobody questions it.

70
00:07:52.420 --> 00:07:57.180
Two P.M. Learn Mandarin via neural download. Takes four minutes.

71
00:07:57.860 --> 00:08:01.100
You already learned Spanish, French, and Klingon yesterday.

72
00:08:01.700 --> 00:08:09.840
Then you spend an hour writing a cookbook for emotions. Today's recipe: the specific feeling of reuniting with someone you

73
00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:16.340
forgot you missed. Pairs well with rain. Your A.I. reminds you about your three P.M.

74
00:08:16.380 --> 00:08:21.960
meeting. You missed it. Nobody cares. The project finished itself using swarm intelligence.

75
00:08:23.420 --> 00:08:28.160
Four P.M. Annual health checkup. Nanobots scanned you while you slept.

76
00:08:28.420 --> 00:08:33.900
They found and eliminated a precancerous cell at three cells, not three billion.

77
00:08:34.679 --> 00:08:38.480
An A.I. ran a billion simulations on your genome because it was bored.

78
00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:43.280
Your gut bacteria got optimized to make you fifteen percent funnier.

79
00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:49.540
Cost: zero dollars. The peace dividend covers it, and nobody had to set up a GoFundMe.

80
00:08:50.800 --> 00:08:58.300
Six P.M. Family dinner. Everyone's here because nobody dies anymore (unless they want to, and that's between them and their

81
00:08:58.300 --> 00:09:04.240
therapist). Your neighborhood voted on tomorrow's weather. Can't agree on which week of autumn.

82
00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:13.260
Planning next week's body modifications: daughter wants wings, son wants gills, you're considering photosynthesis so you

83
00:09:13.260 --> 00:09:19.660
never have to cook again. Nine P.M. Upload today's experiences to collective consciousness.

84
00:09:20.220 --> 00:09:25.540
Download everyone else's. You've now lived eight billion days in one day.

85
00:09:26.380 --> 00:09:32.800
Time is weird. Twitter is tolerable because everyone's right about everything simultaneously.

86
00:09:33.540 --> 00:09:36.940
(This was the hardest problem to solve. Harder than death.).

87
00:09:38.200 --> 00:09:47.140
Ten P.M. Sleep in programmable dream suite. Tonight's dreams designed by Pixar, sponsored by nobody (advertising died when

88
00:09:47.140 --> 00:09:53.290
scarcity did). Your body repairs itself, reverses aging, backs up your consciousness.

89
00:09:54.480 --> 00:09:58.220
Tomorrow you might try being twenty-five again. Or a dolphin.

90
00:09:58.980 --> 00:10:03.860
The dolphins say it's great. The Diseases That Stopped Existing.

91
00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:10.640
You remember these the way you remember dial-up internet. Vaguely horrifying in retrospect.

92
00:10:11.780 --> 00:10:15.860
"You mean people just... had cancer? And they couldn't fix it?

93
00:10:16.480 --> 00:10:21.820
What did they do?" "They held fundraising walks." "They WALKED?

94
00:10:22.340 --> 00:10:27.680
That was the plan?" "It raised awareness." "They were already aware!

95
00:10:27.940 --> 00:10:33.620
They had cancer!". Most cancers: detected at stage zero, eliminated immediately.

96
00:10:34.600 --> 00:10:37.940
The oncology wing of the hospital is now a trampoline park.

97
00:10:39.100 --> 00:10:47.620
Type two diabetes: prevented with personalized nutrition that knows more about your body than you do, which isn't hard because

98
00:10:47.740 --> 00:10:54.740
you don't know anything about your body. Alzheimer's: caught twenty years before symptoms, reversed completely.

99
00:10:55.600 --> 00:11:03.540
Heart disease: arteries cleaned monthly like oil changes, which is a maintenance schedule your species already understands

100
00:11:03.540 --> 00:11:08.560
for cars but somehow never applied to the machine you live inside.

101
00:11:09.120 --> 00:11:14.120
Depression: actually cured, not "have you tried going for a walk?"

102
00:11:14.860 --> 00:11:21.560
(Going for a walk is lovely. It does not cure depression. Nothing that lovely cures anything.

103
00:11:22.040 --> 00:11:31.400
If it did, ice cream would be medicine.) Aging itself: in the first generation, still happens, but forty percent slower,

104
00:11:31.860 --> 00:11:40.800
which means your species' midlife crisis now arrives at seventy instead of forty, giving you thirty extra years to buy the

105
00:11:40.800 --> 00:11:49.970
sports car. In the second generation, it reverses entirely, at which point the sports car becomes a long-term investment.

106
00:11:51.260 --> 00:11:55.860
In the first generation after the treaty, death still exists.

107
00:11:56.040 --> 00:12:03.260
But you die at one hundred and fifty after a good life, not at seventy-five from something that was fixable the whole time.

108
00:12:03.940 --> 00:12:11.800
Give it another generation, and "die" becomes a verb you use ironically, the way you currently use "literally," which is

109
00:12:11.800 --> 00:12:18.020
to say: incorrectly and constantly. The Peace Dividend Economy.

110
00:12:19.400 --> 00:12:23.480
Money still exists. It just flows toward things that don't explode.

111
00:12:24.240 --> 00:12:27.680
(Low bar, but you'd be surprised how long it took you to clear it.).

112
00:12:29.280 --> 00:12:33.180
Greed doesn't disappear (you're still human). It just starts wearing a lab coat.

113
00:12:34.500 --> 00:12:40.620
After the initial one percent treaty proved successful, nations gradually redirected more.

114
00:12:41.200 --> 00:12:45.600
From military budgets: one point three five trillion dollars.

115
00:12:46.560 --> 00:12:51.830
Economic growth from a healthy population: three point five trillion dollars.

116
00:12:51.940 --> 00:12:56.200
Reduced healthcare costs: two point eight trillion dollars.

117
00:12:56.960 --> 00:13:02.300
Productivity gains from people not being dead: four point one trillion dollars.

118
00:13:03.200 --> 00:13:08.600
Total annual peace dividend: eleven point seven five trillion dollars.

119
00:13:09.400 --> 00:13:14.860
That's new wealth generated by the radical innovation of people not being sick or dead.

120
00:13:15.600 --> 00:13:22.040
Alive, healthy people are productive. Who knew? Everyone. Everyone knew.

121
00:13:22.600 --> 00:13:30.840
For centuries. You just kept not doing it, which is the defining characteristic of your species: knowing the right thing

122
00:13:30.840 --> 00:13:37.990
and then not doing it, elaborately, with funding. Here's what that means for your paycheck.

123
00:13:39.200 --> 00:13:44.990
Your species' productivity per person has roughly tripled since nineteen seventy.

124
00:13:45.320 --> 00:13:49.940
Median wages, adjusted for actual living costs, barely moved.

125
00:13:50.680 --> 00:13:59.300
The gap between what you produce and what you earn went to the items listed above: military budgets, administrative overhead,

126
00:13:59.340 --> 00:14:04.260
and a financial system that converts your productivity into other people's assets.

127
00:14:05.160 --> 00:14:09.100
You subsidized your own impoverishment, which takes talent.

128
00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:17.240
After the treaty, when that gap closes, something strange happens: the poorest person alive lives better than any pharaoh.

129
00:14:17.920 --> 00:14:26.680
This is already almost true. The pharaoh had ten thousand servants but no antibiotics, no air conditioning, no anesthesia,

130
00:14:26.680 --> 00:14:34.160
and no dentistry worth discussing. He died at forty of something your cheapest clinic could fix between lunch and a meeting.

131
00:14:34.900 --> 00:14:39.360
You already live better than the richest human who ever existed before nineteen hundred.

132
00:14:39.900 --> 00:14:46.840
The peace dividend extends that logic forward: your great-grandchildren will look at twenty twenty-six billionaires the

133
00:14:46.840 --> 00:14:50.580
way you look at pharaohs. Rich by the standards of their time.

134
00:14:50.920 --> 00:14:58.160
Impoverished by any standard that matters. On Wishonia, your current billionaires would fall below the poverty line.

135
00:14:58.280 --> 00:15:01.200
They'd be featured in guilt-inducing late-night commercials.

136
00:15:01.720 --> 00:15:08.560
"For just pennies a day, you can help this billionaire experience a single Tuesday without existential dread.

137
00:15:09.160 --> 00:15:16.400
He has a yacht, but his serotonin is produced by a two hundred thousand-year-old algorithm that doesn't know the yacht exists.

138
00:15:17.140 --> 00:15:23.540
Please. He's suffering in a very expensive way.". The peace dividend doesn't make you rich.

139
00:15:23.900 --> 00:15:28.800
It stops making you poor. That's compound interest on not being robbed.

140
00:15:29.420 --> 00:15:33.960
On Wishonia, we don't have a word for this because we never invented the problem.

141
00:15:34.620 --> 00:15:42.660
The closest translation is "stopping the crime and returning the money," which on your planet is apparently a radical economic

142
00:15:42.660 --> 00:15:47.820
proposal rather than the obvious thing. The money doesn't trickle down.

143
00:15:48.140 --> 00:15:55.900
("Trickle-down economics" is a phrase your species uses to describe money that flows upward, which is the most honest mislabeling

144
00:15:55.900 --> 00:16:05.460
since "Department of Defense.") It flows to every citizen directly as a universal basic income, denominated in wishes, deposited

145
00:16:05.460 --> 00:16:11.120
daily via smart contract. No application form. No means testing.

146
00:16:11.560 --> 00:16:15.460
No bureaucrat deciding whether your poverty is the deserving kind.

147
00:16:16.020 --> 00:16:23.940
The funding mechanism: a flat transaction tax on all economic activity, collected automatically at the point of transaction

148
00:16:24.040 --> 00:16:30.990
by the same smart contracts that distribute the U.B.I. The savings from this alone are staggering.

149
00:16:31.500 --> 00:16:38.700
Your species currently spends five hundred and forty-six billion dollars per year on the *process* of collecting taxes:

150
00:16:39.220 --> 00:16:46.020
accountants, software, seven point nine billion hours of lost productivity, and an I.R.S.

151
00:16:46.020 --> 00:16:51.980
that employs tens of thousands of people to do what a smart contract does at the speed of light for free.

152
00:16:53.120 --> 00:17:00.060
Then another five hundred and eighty billion dollars per year on regulatory compliance: filling out forms to prove you filled

153
00:17:00.060 --> 00:17:07.560
out the previous forms, hiring people whose entire job is translating government requirements into other government requirements,

154
00:17:08.020 --> 00:17:12.640
and maintaining compliance departments larger than the teams doing the actual work.

155
00:17:13.460 --> 00:17:21.880
That's over a trillion dollars per year on the process of *permission.* Then you spend tens of billions more on the *process*

156
00:17:21.880 --> 00:17:29.540
of giving some of the money back: Social Security Administration, food stamp offices, housing voucher programs, disability

157
00:17:29.540 --> 00:17:37.280
determination centers, each with their own buildings, employees, forms, appeals processes, and catering budgets.

158
00:17:38.340 --> 00:17:47.560
The flat tax and U.B.I. eliminate all of it. Every dollar previously spent processing poverty now goes to eliminating it.

159
00:17:48.320 --> 00:17:56.900
Total waste in the U.S. system alone: four point nine trillion dollars per year, or seventeen percent of G.D.P.

160
00:17:57.340 --> 00:18:06.140
That's not a rounding error. That's the entire economy of Japan being fed into a shredder annually, and the shredder has

161
00:18:06.140 --> 00:18:10.100
a compliance department that also needs to be fed into a larger shredder.

162
00:18:10.760 --> 00:18:14.040
The administrative savings alone would fund a meaningful U.B.I.

163
00:18:15.180 --> 00:18:20.180
increase, which is the most expensive free money your species has ever discovered.

164
00:18:20.980 --> 00:18:27.580
But the U.B.I. is just cash. The real revolution is what happens when you apply the same principle to everything government

165
00:18:27.580 --> 00:18:35.300
currently distributes badly. Every child gets an education credit, deposited annually, spendable at any accredited school.

166
00:18:35.800 --> 00:18:42.800
The money follows the student, not the institution. Bad schools don't get shut down by a bureaucrat who visited once and

167
00:18:42.800 --> 00:18:50.000
filled out a rubric. They get shut down by parents who stopped choosing them, which is faster, more accurate, and doesn't

168
00:18:50.000 --> 00:18:54.340
require a rubric. Good schools grow. Great schools get copied.

169
00:18:54.940 --> 00:19:01.770
The Department of Education goes the way of the I.R.S.: replaced by an algorithm that tracks outcomes and accredits providers,

170
00:19:01.900 --> 00:19:04.980
not a ministry that tracks compliance and accredits paperwork.

171
00:19:05.680 --> 00:19:11.070
Healthcare works the same way: every citizen gets a health credit, spendable at any provider.

172
00:19:11.480 --> 00:19:17.920
No insurance company standing between you and your doctor, extracting twenty percent for the privilege of saying "no" to

173
00:19:17.920 --> 00:19:25.040
your claims. No hospital charging fifty dollars for aspirin because the pricing is hidden behind seven layers of bureaucratic

174
00:19:25.040 --> 00:19:33.600
abstraction. Your current healthcare system wastes one point two trillion dollars per year on administrative overhead alone:

175
00:19:34.120 --> 00:19:40.280
billing departments larger than medical staffs, prior authorization forms that take longer to fill out than the procedures

176
00:19:40.280 --> 00:19:47.140
they authorize, and an insurance industry whose primary function is adding cost to the process of paying costs.

177
00:19:47.960 --> 00:19:53.970
You see the price. You choose the provider. The provider that keeps you healthiest for the least money wins.

178
00:19:54.840 --> 00:20:02.360
This is what your economists call "a market," and it works everywhere your species has tried it except in the two sectors

179
00:20:02.360 --> 00:20:10.120
where you decided to replace it with committees, which are, coincidentally, the two sectors with the fastest-rising costs

180
00:20:10.120 --> 00:20:18.120
and the most paperwork: education and healthcare. On Wishonia, we noticed this coincidence four thousand years ago.

181
00:20:18.500 --> 00:20:23.620
It took your economists slightly longer. The poverty line isn't a policy target.

182
00:20:24.360 --> 00:20:29.860
It's a floor the system physically cannot go below, the way an elevator has a ground floor.

183
00:20:30.360 --> 00:20:37.470
You can debate whether the floor should be higher (it should; on Wishonia, the floor is "comfortable," not "surviving").

184
00:20:38.340 --> 00:20:44.960
You cannot debate whether it should exist. The algorithm doesn't have a "let people starve" setting.

185
00:20:45.020 --> 00:20:50.260
On Wishonia, we find it remarkable that yours does. Your Corporate Heroes.

186
00:20:51.080 --> 00:20:58.680
Same companies that made weapons now make life. Guidance systems, targeting algorithms, precision engineering: all very

187
00:20:58.680 --> 00:21:04.240
useful when the target is cancer instead of a village. The engineering is identical.

188
00:21:04.280 --> 00:21:11.000
The job satisfaction is improved. Lockheed Martin Health: Cancer-detecting satellites spot tumors from orbit.

189
00:21:11.640 --> 00:21:19.280
Stock price: twelve thousand dollars per share. Shareholders discover that alive customers buy more things than dead ones,

190
00:21:19.640 --> 00:21:25.880
which is a business insight so basic that it took your species seventy years of weapons manufacturing to figure out.

191
00:21:26.280 --> 00:21:30.900
Raytheon Wellness. Missile guidance systems make excellent surgical robots.

192
00:21:31.560 --> 00:21:35.860
Fifty million perfect surgeries performed. Zero villages destroyed.

193
00:21:36.440 --> 00:21:41.480
A personal best. Boston Dynamics. Robots help elderly walk.

194
00:21:41.920 --> 00:21:50.280
The dancing is still cute, now therapeutic. They finally found a use for the dancing that justifies the billions spent developing

195
00:21:50.280 --> 00:21:57.080
it, which is more than the previous use case could say (the previous use case was "going viral on the internet and making

196
00:21:57.080 --> 00:22:00.880
people nervous about the future"). Palantir Health Insights.

197
00:22:01.960 --> 00:22:05.420
Data analysis predicts disease outbreaks before they happen.

198
00:22:06.040 --> 00:22:12.040
Privacy-protected, transparent, saves lives. Still creepy, but helpfully creepy.

199
00:22:12.880 --> 00:22:16.680
Like a doctor who memorizes your schedule and shows up before you know you're sick.

200
00:22:17.220 --> 00:22:22.700
You appreciate it and also you lock your doors. Your New Problems.

201
00:22:23.360 --> 00:22:27.320
You still have problems. They're just embarrassingly good problems.

202
00:22:27.880 --> 00:22:35.000
Too many healthy one hundred-year-olds want to work. Government crisis meetings about citizens being "too productive and

203
00:22:35.000 --> 00:22:41.920
won't stop contributing to society." This is the kind of emergency that your current politicians would trade their entire

204
00:22:41.920 --> 00:22:46.540
career to have, and they will, eventually, once the treaty passes.

205
00:22:47.260 --> 00:22:52.780
When you live to one hundred and fifty, universities offer "Century Degrees."

206
00:22:52.820 --> 00:22:59.480
One hundred years of education. Finally, enough time to finish a P.H.D.

207
00:22:59.480 --> 00:23:06.220
(Current P.H.D.s take an average of seven years. At that rate, a Century Degree takes fourteen centuries.

208
00:23:06.960 --> 00:23:10.180
The math doesn't work, but neither does academia, so it fits.).

209
00:23:11.080 --> 00:23:14.220
When you're not worried about dying, you worry about meaning.

210
00:23:14.760 --> 00:23:18.880
Philosophy becomes the fastest-growing major. Philosophers are thrilled.

211
00:23:19.800 --> 00:23:25.040
This is the first time anyone has used "philosophers" and "thrilled" in the same sentence.

212
00:23:25.640 --> 00:23:30.220
The philosophy department gets a window. Several philosophers faint.

213
00:23:31.240 --> 00:23:36.640
These are luxury problems. You'll take them over cancer. What Fixing Health Fixed.

214
00:23:37.480 --> 00:23:43.440
Nobody predicted this. (I predicted this.) Fixing health fixed everything else.

215
00:23:43.740 --> 00:23:48.060
Like pulling one thread and the whole sweater of human misery unravels.

216
00:23:48.540 --> 00:23:55.880
Climate. Climate change: solved the moment everyone realized they'd personally be alive to see the consequences.

217
00:23:56.860 --> 00:24:02.940
Amazing how much you care about sea levels when your beach house needs to last one hundred and fifty years instead of thirty.

218
00:24:04.080 --> 00:24:08.880
It also helped that you stopped spending fifty billion dollars per year, I.M.F.

219
00:24:08.940 --> 00:24:13.560
fossil fuel twenty twenty-three, subsidizing the industry that was causing the problem.

220
00:24:14.380 --> 00:24:21.260
"Fossil fuel subsidy" is a phrase that means "paying someone to set your house on fire and then paying a second person to

221
00:24:21.260 --> 00:24:27.980
study why houses keep catching fire." Turns out the problem was never that humans couldn't see the future.

222
00:24:28.480 --> 00:24:32.730
It's that they didn't expect to be there for it, and also they were paying the arsonists.

223
00:24:34.760 --> 00:24:40.500
Poverty. Poverty: global G.D.P. tripled when you stopped losing productive years to preventable disease.

224
00:24:41.080 --> 00:24:47.420
But the health dividend was only the beginning. The policies that kept people poor couldn't survive without politicians

225
00:24:47.420 --> 00:24:55.780
to defend them. Your zoning laws alone destroyed one point four trillion dollars per year in economic output by making it

226
00:24:55.880 --> 00:25:03.020
illegal to build housing where people needed it. You had a housing crisis and a solution (build houses), and your primary

227
00:25:03.020 --> 00:25:10.020
governmental response was to ban the solution so existing homeowners could watch their property values rise while their

228
00:25:10.020 --> 00:25:17.820
children moved back in. You spent seventy-five billion dollars per year paying farmers not to grow food while people went

229
00:25:17.820 --> 00:25:24.280
hungry, which is the kind of policy that sounds like satire until you look it up and discover it's real, and then it still

230
00:25:24.280 --> 00:25:28.850
sounds like satire, which is the most damning thing you can say about an agricultural policy.

231
00:25:29.620 --> 00:25:36.400
And one hundred and sixty billion dollars per year taxing yourselves for buying things, which is economically identical

232
00:25:36.400 --> 00:25:39.520
to punching yourself in the face and then complaining about the bruise.

233
00:25:40.100 --> 00:25:44.190
Nobody would voluntarily fund any of this, which is why it required middlemen.

234
00:25:45.020 --> 00:25:49.740
When citizens allocated directly, the policies evaporated and the G.D.P.

235
00:25:49.920 --> 00:25:53.680
that was being destroyed by strategic self-sabotage came back.

236
00:25:54.520 --> 00:26:02.900
Economists are furious that nobody asked them. Science. Clinical trials: when you're not spending eighty percent of research

237
00:26:02.900 --> 00:26:07.200
budgets on grant applications, scientists actually do science.

238
00:26:07.980 --> 00:26:12.900
Pragmatic trial enrollment hit ninety-nine point nine percent of eligible patients.

239
00:26:13.440 --> 00:26:19.840
The remaining zero point one percent are holding out for philosophical reasons that the newly thrilled philosophy department

240
00:26:19.840 --> 00:26:24.780
is still debating. Innovation: healthy brains think better.

241
00:26:25.260 --> 00:26:29.280
More breakthrough discoveries in twenty years than the previous two hundred.

242
00:26:29.800 --> 00:26:37.300
It's almost as if not poisoning your scientists with stress, sleep deprivation, and grant-writing improves their output.

243
00:26:37.320 --> 00:26:42.000
On Wishonia, this observation is four thousand two hundred and ninety-seven years old.

244
00:26:42.620 --> 00:26:51.280
On Earth, it's still somehow controversial. Peace. War is a principal-agent problem, and wishocracy eliminated the agent.

245
00:26:51.800 --> 00:26:55.460
Your citizens never directly voted to start any war in the last century.

246
00:26:56.120 --> 00:27:02.180
Politicians voted for wars because the defense industry funded their campaigns, and the defense industry funded campaigns

247
00:27:02.180 --> 00:27:08.960
because politicians voted for wars. Your political scientists had a name for this feedback loop (the military-industrial

248
00:27:08.960 --> 00:27:15.840
complex), and the man who named it (Eisenhower) was running the largest military in history at the time, which tells you

249
00:27:15.840 --> 00:27:20.120
everything about the difference between diagnosing a disease and curing it.

250
00:27:20.160 --> 00:27:25.020
Remove the politicians, and you remove the only people who personally profited from war.

251
00:27:25.760 --> 00:27:33.000
Six hundred and fifteen billion dollars per year in military spending beyond what actual defense required evaporated overnight,

252
00:27:33.600 --> 00:27:39.900
because when eight billion people allocate directly, nobody checks the box marked "more bombs than the next ten countries

253
00:27:39.900 --> 00:27:45.400
combined." The peace dividend then did the rest: every citizen getting a daily U.B.I.

254
00:27:45.620 --> 00:27:52.480
deposit, every bondholder earning returns from health outcomes, every corporation that pivoted to biotech has a direct,

255
00:27:52.960 --> 00:28:00.600
measurable financial stake in continued peace. Re-arming means losing returns that eight billion people can see on the wishocracy

256
00:28:00.980 --> 00:28:07.070
dashboard in real time. Peace stopped being a moral aspiration and became a Nash equilibrium.

257
00:28:07.700 --> 00:28:12.400
The game theory was always there. You just needed to remove the players who were rigging the game.

258
00:28:12.940 --> 00:28:19.480
Governance. Politicians went extinct. Not violently (that would have been on-brand for your species, but no).

259
00:28:20.040 --> 00:28:27.290
Their ecological niche disappeared. A politician is a middleman between citizens and resource allocation.

260
00:28:27.540 --> 00:28:35.160
Wishocracy lets citizens allocate directly, the way the internet let travelers book flights without calling a person whose

261
00:28:35.160 --> 00:28:38.760
entire job was talking to a different computer on your behalf.

262
00:28:39.560 --> 00:28:50.130
The last politicians tried to ban wishocracy. The ban was allocated zero percent of the budget by eight billion people simultaneously,

263
00:28:50.860 --> 00:28:56.790
which was the most democratic thing that had ever happened in the history of democracy and also the funniest.

264
00:28:57.760 --> 00:29:06.280
Then the food chain collapsed. Lobbyists were an invasive species that had evolved to feed exclusively on politicians.

265
00:29:06.780 --> 00:29:09.700
Remove the host species, the parasite has nothing to parasitize.

266
00:29:11.240 --> 00:29:17.480
four point four billion dollars per year in lobbying spending became zero dollars overnight.

267
00:29:18.200 --> 00:29:25.240
So did the one hundred and eighty-one billion dollars per year in corporate welfare that the lobbying was purchasing, because

268
00:29:25.240 --> 00:29:30.960
it turns out companies stop asking the government for free money when there's nobody at the government to ask.

269
00:29:31.700 --> 00:29:38.760
The lobbyists became life coaches. The career transition was seamless; the skill set was identical (tell powerful people

270
00:29:38.760 --> 00:29:42.800
what they want to hear, charge by the hour). The think tanks followed.

271
00:29:43.040 --> 00:29:45.960
Then the political consultants. Then the cable news pundits.

272
00:29:47.100 --> 00:29:55.860
An entire ecosystem, evolved over centuries to convert public money into private influence, collapsed in a decade because

273
00:29:55.860 --> 00:30:03.620
the supply of corruptible middlemen dried up. Ecologists called it the fastest mass extinction since the dinosaurs.

274
00:30:04.220 --> 00:30:08.520
The dinosaurs had an asteroid. The politicians just had a better app.

275
00:30:10.500 --> 00:30:14.860
Happiness. Not dying of preventable diseases makes people happier.

276
00:30:15.320 --> 00:30:22.440
I apologize for stating something so obvious. But given that you spent four thousand years not acting on it, apparently

277
00:30:22.440 --> 00:30:29.920
it needed saying. It needed saying slowly, in a manual, with pictures, and financial incentives.

278
00:30:30.920 --> 00:30:40.020
This is your species. I work with what I have. Freedom. This was the one nobody expected.

279
00:30:40.300 --> 00:30:48.500
Not even me. (I expected everything else. This surprised even Wishonia.) When your biotechnology got good enough to cure

280
00:30:48.500 --> 00:30:53.840
diseases, it got good enough to understand why you couldn't think straight in the first place.

281
00:30:54.740 --> 00:31:01.700
Ninety-five percent of your decisions were being made by a two hundred thousand-year-old survival algorithm that didn't

282
00:31:01.700 --> 00:31:09.220
speak your language. Your violence module kept you building weapons for threats that stopped being relevant when you stopped

283
00:31:09.220 --> 00:31:16.660
living in caves. Your tribal brain could handle one hundred and fifty relationships, and you asked it to run a civilization

284
00:31:16.660 --> 00:31:26.800
of eight billion. You were a species with godlike technology and hamster-level impulse control, governed entirely by vibes.

285
00:31:27.280 --> 00:31:32.920
The same precision therapies that cured depression gave you admin access to the operating system.

286
00:31:33.440 --> 00:31:40.840
Not overriding your emotions (that was tried in the twenty-thirties; it went badly; everyone became very productive and

287
00:31:40.840 --> 00:31:46.260
very empty, like a corporation with no employees). Modulating them.

288
00:31:46.340 --> 00:31:49.980
The violence module isn't deleted. It's given an off switch.

289
00:31:50.600 --> 00:31:55.340
You can still feel protective anger; you just can't be hijacked by it in a voting booth.

290
00:31:56.180 --> 00:32:04.700
Tribal loyalty isn't removed. It's expanded. When your neurochemistry stops screaming "stranger equals danger" at every

291
00:32:04.700 --> 00:32:11.540
face that doesn't look like your one hundred and fifty-person in-group, cooperation with strangers stops feeling like a

292
00:32:11.540 --> 00:32:15.920
sacrifice and starts feeling like the obvious thing it always was.

293
00:32:16.400 --> 00:32:23.260
Not because you became saints. Because you stopped running software that made sainthood neurochemically impossible.

294
00:32:23.680 --> 00:32:31.240
But the real revolution wasn't turning off bad impulses. It was making good ones feel just as good.

295
00:32:31.820 --> 00:32:37.780
For your entire existence as a species, being moral required fighting your own brain.

296
00:32:38.120 --> 00:32:44.130
The people who managed it, you called saints. The people who failed (everyone), you called human.

297
00:32:44.940 --> 00:32:53.480
The difference was never character. It was neurochemistry. Willpower is a finite resource; your brain budgets it like calories,

298
00:32:53.720 --> 00:33:01.800
and it runs out by Thursday. So you spent millennia writing moral codes that your own hardware wouldn't let you follow,

299
00:33:02.100 --> 00:33:05.400
then feeling guilty about it, which your religions monetized.

300
00:33:05.920 --> 00:33:10.860
Efficient, in a depressing way. Now you choose what feels rewarding.

301
00:33:11.620 --> 00:33:18.600
A person whose brain used to reward aggression discovers that saving a life delivers the same dopamine cascade.

302
00:33:19.300 --> 00:33:25.580
Someone inclined to hoard finds that building housing for strangers feels the way acquisition used to.

303
00:33:26.220 --> 00:33:33.880
Your religious people, who spent two millennia trying to be Christlike using a brain that neurochemically punishes selflessness,

304
00:33:34.080 --> 00:33:38.850
can finally feel as good being generous as they used to feel being selfish.

305
00:33:39.680 --> 00:33:48.480
Altruism stops being sacrifice and starts being appetite. Self-control stops being a war with yourself and becomes a preference

306
00:33:48.480 --> 00:33:56.380
setting. Nobody makes you do any of this. That bears repeating because your species has a long history of people with power

307
00:33:56.380 --> 00:34:04.060
deciding what other people should feel. On Wishonia, we replaced representative government with constitutional wishocracy

308
00:34:04.060 --> 00:34:12.159
four millennia ago. There are no politicians left to mandate anything, even if mandating neurochemical changes were something

309
00:34:12.159 --> 00:34:20.580
a sane civilization would permit, which it is not. The constitution has one constraint on self-modification: the word "self."

310
00:34:21.380 --> 00:34:27.280
You modify your own neurochemistry. Nobody else's. Not by majority vote.

311
00:34:27.380 --> 00:34:32.409
Not by executive order. Not by a committee that met once and felt productive about it.

312
00:34:32.580 --> 00:34:39.679
Your species will find this alarmingly permissive until you realize it's just bodily autonomy applied consistently, which

313
00:34:39.679 --> 00:34:43.600
is something you already claim to support but have never actually tried.

314
00:34:44.500 --> 00:34:52.600
(Your previous policy on neurochemical self-determination was the war on drugs: ninety billion dollars per year at drug

315
00:34:52.600 --> 00:34:59.300
policy alliance twenty twenty-one criminalizing some molecules while advertising others during football games.

316
00:34:59.760 --> 00:35:04.510
The classification system was not based on pharmacology. It was based on who had lobbyists.

317
00:35:05.140 --> 00:35:14.120
This is not a policy. This is astrology with handcuffs.). On Wishonia, this is when the arguments about "human nature" finally

318
00:35:14.120 --> 00:35:19.300
ended. Human nature was never the problem. Human neurochemistry was.

319
00:35:19.300 --> 00:35:25.820
And neurochemistry is just engineering with smaller parts. The selfish gene is still selfish.

320
00:35:26.140 --> 00:35:32.180
You just stopped letting it drive. The whole time, you were passengers in a vehicle you thought you were piloting.

321
00:35:32.820 --> 00:35:39.620
Now you actually have the wheel. What you do with it is up to you, which is the first time that sentence has been true for

322
00:35:39.620 --> 00:35:46.780
any member of your species. The Three Supers Complete. Super-Intelligence: complete.

323
00:35:47.480 --> 00:35:55.140
Every human accesses all knowledge instantly. Arguments still happen, but now they're about interesting things instead of

324
00:35:55.140 --> 00:36:01.040
things that were settled by evidence centuries ago. The "gif" question was finally settled.

325
00:36:01.960 --> 00:36:06.030
Several nations cited this as their primary reason for signing the treaty.

326
00:36:06.240 --> 00:36:14.000
Super-Longevity: solved. Death is like moving to Ohio. Technically possible but nobody does it voluntarily.

327
00:36:14.780 --> 00:36:20.220
Some choose to die for artistic reasons. They get better. Death sues for wrongful termination.

328
00:36:20.640 --> 00:36:24.080
Case dismissed. The judge is three hundred and forty years old and has no sympathy.

329
00:36:25.400 --> 00:36:31.880
Super-Wellbeing: suffering exists only in history classes. Students don't believe it was real.

330
00:36:32.480 --> 00:36:38.140
"People felt bad without choosing to? That's insane." "They also fought wars."

331
00:36:38.420 --> 00:36:43.660
"What's a war?" "It's where you organize millions of people to destroy things and kill each other."

332
00:36:44.240 --> 00:36:49.030
"On purpose?" "On purpose." "And they had the Three Supers?"

333
00:36:49.620 --> 00:36:53.580
"No, they spent the money on the killing part." Long pause.

334
00:36:54.160 --> 00:36:59.600
"Were they stupid?" This is the question your great-great-grandchildren will ask.

335
00:37:00.140 --> 00:37:07.380
The answer is no. You weren't stupid. You were just slaves to an incentive structure that made stupidity profitable.

336
00:37:08.000 --> 00:37:12.680
This manual fixes the incentive structure. The stupidity will resolve itself.

337
00:37:13.220 --> 00:37:17.820
The last military on Earth (North Korea, obviously) finally disbands.

338
00:37:18.540 --> 00:37:22.740
They were defending against an enemy that stopped existing forty years ago.

339
00:37:23.120 --> 00:37:27.420
The soldiers become interpretive dancers. They're surprisingly good.

340
00:37:28.240 --> 00:37:35.340
Your Personal Future. Your health: live to one hundred and fifty plus, or forever.

341
00:37:35.500 --> 00:37:43.500
Debug your body, upgrade it, back it up. You will see technologies that make the smartphone look like a rock, and the smartphone

342
00:37:43.500 --> 00:37:50.700
already makes most of your species' previous technologies look like rocks, so the rock-to-rock comparison should give you

343
00:37:50.700 --> 00:37:54.990
some sense of scale. Your family: you'll meet your great-great-great-grandchildren.

344
00:37:57.140 --> 00:38:04.520
Your dog is immortal and possibly telepathic. You finally have enough time to read all those books you bought and stacked

345
00:38:04.520 --> 00:38:08.920
on your nightstand in twenty twenty-four when you were pretending to be someone who reads.

346
00:38:09.500 --> 00:38:13.560
Your purpose: with survival handled, you focus on what matters.

347
00:38:14.300 --> 00:38:18.680
Creating universes. Solving entropy. Perfecting the sandwich.

348
00:38:19.380 --> 00:38:23.940
The sandwich faction is the largest research group in the post-scarcity economy.

349
00:38:24.580 --> 00:38:32.080
This surprised no one. The sandwich is humanity's most important invention and the one it takes least seriously, which is

350
00:38:32.080 --> 00:38:37.240
backwards, and fixing this is the first priority of the Wishonian post-death era.

351
00:38:37.680 --> 00:38:42.380
What Comes After. You solved death, suffering, and scarcity.

352
00:38:43.080 --> 00:38:48.380
Now what? Some humans explore galaxies. Some become pure consciousness.

353
00:38:48.820 --> 00:38:56.670
Some perfect the sandwich. Your network of decentralized institutes of health still exists, but it ran out of diseases.

354
00:38:57.320 --> 00:39:04.160
It pivots to making reality more interesting. Today's project: teaching gravity to be less clingy.

355
00:39:04.840 --> 00:39:11.680
Gravity is not cooperating. Gravity has been uncooperative for thirteen point eight billion years.

356
00:39:12.120 --> 00:39:17.640
It shows no signs of changing. The research continues. The universe notices you.

357
00:39:18.060 --> 00:39:23.200
It's impressed. You're invited to the Galactic Council. You bring potato salad.

358
00:39:23.300 --> 00:39:29.780
Everyone loves it. Potato salad saves the universe. This is not how anyone expected the story to end.

359
00:39:30.180 --> 00:39:37.380
But it's how it ends. You're still alive to see it. Because you followed the instructions in this manual.

360
00:39:38.300 --> 00:39:42.500
I should mention: everything in this chapter is the simplified version.

361
00:39:42.820 --> 00:39:51.200
The real Wishonia, after four thousand two hundred and ninety-seven years of compound progress with zero waste, is as far

362
00:39:51.200 --> 00:39:55.620
beyond what I just described as what I described is beyond your current Tuesday.

363
00:39:56.460 --> 00:40:03.960
I told you about Body O.S. updates, I.Q. sliders, and nanobots because those are concepts your brain can process.

364
00:40:04.520 --> 00:40:13.500
The actual experience of a Wishonian Tuesday is closer to a constant stream of novel peak experience, unimaginable gradients

365
00:40:13.500 --> 00:40:20.640
of bliss, and a depth of connection with other beings that your language doesn't have a word for because your neurochemistry

366
00:40:20.640 --> 00:40:24.880
doesn't have a setting for it. I described the version you can picture.

367
00:40:25.360 --> 00:40:31.900
The real version requires the upgrade first. But the version you can picture is already better than what you have.

368
00:40:32.600 --> 00:40:39.760
So start there. Your Two Paths. You're at the fork. Path A: The Dystopia.

369
00:40:40.160 --> 00:40:43.380
You spent trillions on death. Got exactly what you paid for.

370
00:40:44.140 --> 00:40:52.680
Path B: Wishonia. You spent one percent on life. My planet chose Path B four thousand two hundred and ninety-seven years

371
00:40:52.680 --> 00:40:57.260
ago. We're doing fine. The math is simple. Even for humans.

372
00:40:58.000 --> 00:41:05.380
(Especially for humans, because you invented math and then refused to use it.) The United States alone wastes one hundred

373
00:41:05.380 --> 00:41:11.020
and eighty times more on governmental dysfunction than the entire treaty would cost.

374
00:41:11.860 --> 00:41:18.880
You are not being asked to find new money. You are being asked to stop losing the money you already have.

375
00:41:20.020 --> 00:41:24.760
While you read this chapter, over two thousand people died of preventable diseases.

376
00:41:25.680 --> 00:41:33.240
(One hundred and fifty thousand per day equals one hundred and four per minute.) They did not get a vote on which path you

377
00:41:33.240 --> 00:41:44.560
chose. There's no Door C. Pick one. Wishonia World Integrated System for High-Efficiency Optimization, Networked Intelligence,

378
00:41:44.620 --> 00:41:47.100
and Allocation Bring Potato Salad
